---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SalaryCalculator from '@components/islands/SalaryCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'salary-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I convert an hourly wage to an annual salary?', answer: 'Multiply your hourly rate by the hours you work per week, then by the weeks you work per year. For example, $25/hour × 40 hours × 52 weeks = $52,000 per year. This calculator does it in every direction.' },
  { question: 'How do I convert an annual salary to an hourly rate?', answer: 'Divide the annual salary by the hours you work in a year. At a standard 40 hours a week for 52 weeks (2,080 hours), a $60,000 salary is 60,000 ÷ 2,080 ≈ $28.85 per hour.' },
  { question: 'What is an annual salary?', answer: 'Annual salary is your total pay for a year of work, usually quoted before tax (gross). It is a fixed figure for salaried roles, whereas hourly workers earn their rate times the hours actually worked.' },
  { question: 'How many work hours are in a year?', answer: 'A common full-time assumption is 2,080 hours (40 hours × 52 weeks). Adjust the hours-per-week and weeks-per-year fields to match your actual schedule, including unpaid time off.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between gross and net salary?', answer: 'Gross salary is your pay before deductions; net (take-home) pay is what lands in your account after income tax, payroll taxes and any benefits or retirement contributions. These figures are gross.' },
  { question: 'Is this gross or net pay?', answer: 'These figures are gross (before tax). To estimate what you keep, use the income tax calculator.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="Convert pay between hourly, daily, weekly, biweekly, monthly and annual figures — enter any one and see them all." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-10' }}>
  <SalaryCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter a <strong>pay amount</strong> — any single figure you already know.</li>
      <li>Choose the <strong>period</strong> that amount is quoted in: per hour, day, week, biweekly, month or year.</li>
      <li>Adjust the schedule assumptions — <strong>hours per week</strong>, <strong>days per week</strong> and <strong>weeks per year</strong> — to match how you actually work (the defaults are 40, 5 and 52).</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Convert</strong> to fill in every other equivalent at once.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the conversion works</h2>
    <p>
      Job offers arrive in different units — one hourly, one annual, one monthly — which makes
      them hard to compare. The tool solves this by first turning whatever you enter into a single
      <strong>annual</strong> figure, then dividing that annual total back down into every other
      period. Fixing everything to the yearly total is what keeps the six figures consistent with
      one another.
    </p>

    <h2>The 2,080-hour year</h2>
    <p>
      A standard full-time year is <strong>40 hours × 52 weeks = 2,080 hours</strong>, and the two
      headline conversions follow directly from it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hourly → annual:</strong> rate × hours per week × weeks per year. A $25/hour job at 40 × 52 is $52,000/year.</li>
      <li><strong>Annual → hourly:</strong> salary ÷ yearly hours. A $60,000 salary ÷ 2,080 ≈ $28.85/hour.</li>
      <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> annual ÷ 12. <strong>Weekly:</strong> annual ÷ weeks per year. <strong>Daily:</strong> the weekly figure ÷ days per week.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Because those middle steps depend on your hours, days and weeks, changing the schedule
      changes the hourly and daily figures even when the annual salary stays the same.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take a <strong>$25/hour</strong> job on the default 40 hours, 5 days, 52 weeks. The annual
      figure is 25 × 40 × 52 = <strong>$52,000</strong>. Dividing that back down gives
      <strong>$4,333.33</strong> a month, <strong>$2,000</strong> biweekly, <strong>$1,000</strong>
      a week, and <strong>$200</strong> a day — and, closing the loop, $1,000 ÷ 40 hours brings you
      right back to $25 an hour.
    </p>

    <h2>Quick salary-to-hourly reference</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Annual salary</th><th>Hourly (40 hrs/week)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$30,000</td><td>$14.42</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$40,000</td><td>$19.23</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$50,000</td><td>$24.04</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$60,000</td><td>$28.85</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$75,000</td><td>$36.06</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$100,000</td><td>$48.08</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Biweekly vs. semimonthly vs. monthly</h2>
    <p>
      Watch the paycheck period carefully, because "twice a month" and "every two weeks" are not
      the same. This tool's <strong>biweekly</strong> figure divides the annual salary by 26 — the
      26 paychecks you receive when paid every two weeks. A <strong>semimonthly</strong> schedule
      (the 1st and 15th, say) pays only 24 times a year, so each of those checks is larger, and a
      <strong>monthly</strong> salary divides by 12. On $52,000 that is $2,000 biweekly, about
      $2,166.67 semimonthly, and $4,333.33 monthly.
    </p>

    <h2>Match it to your real schedule</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion is only as accurate as its assumptions. Adjust hours per week, days per week
      and weeks worked per year to reflect overtime, part-time hours or unpaid leave — someone
      working 37.5 hours over 48 paid weeks lands on a very different hourly figure than the 2,080
      default. Every figure here is <strong>gross</strong> pay, before income tax, payroll tax or
      benefits are deducted.
    </p>
    <p>
      To estimate what you actually keep, try the <a href="/finance/income-tax-calculator">income
      tax calculator</a>; to add up time actually worked, see the
      <a href="/everyday/hours-calculator">hours calculator</a>. These are gross figures for
      general use, not payroll or tax advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
